NNAAMMEE
     llaamm — laminate files

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     llaamm [--ff _m_i_n._m_a_x] [--ss _s_e_p_s_t_r_i_n_g] [--tt _c] _f_i_l_e _._._.
     llaamm [--pp _m_i_n._m_a_x] [--ss _s_e_p_s_t_r_i_n_g] [--tt _c] _f_i_l_e _._._.

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The llaamm utility copies the named files side by side onto the standard
     output.  The _n_-_t_h input lines from the input _f_i_l_e_s are considered frag‐
     ments of the single long _n_-_t_h output line into which they are assembled.
     The name `--' means the standard input, and may be repeated.

     --ff _m_i_n._m_a_x, --FF _m_i_n._m_a_x
             Print line fragments according to the format string _m_i_n._m_a_x,
             where _m_i_n is the minimum field width and _m_a_x the maximum field
             width.  If _m_i_n begins with a zero, zeros will be added to make up
             the field width, and if it begins with a `-', the fragment will
             be left-adjusted within the field.  Using --ff applies only to the
             next file while --FF applies to all subsequent files until it ap‐
             pears again uncapitalized.

     --pp _m_i_n._m_a_x, --PP _m_i_n._m_a_x
             Like --ff, but pad this file's field when end-of-file is reached
             and other files are still active.  Using --pp applies only to the
             next file while --PP applies to all subsequent files until it ap‐
             pears again uncapitalized.

     --ss _s_e_p_s_t_r_i_n_g, --SS _s_e_p_s_t_r_i_n_g
             Print _s_e_p_s_t_r_i_n_g before printing line fragments from the next
             file.  This option may appear after the last file.  Using --ss ap‐
             plies only to the next file while --SS applies to all subsequent
             files until it appears again uncapitalized.

     --tt _c, --TT _c
             The input line terminator is _c instead of a newline.  The newline
             normally appended to each output line is omitted.  Using --tt ap‐
             plies only to the next file while --TT applies to all subsequent
             files until it appears again uncapitalized.

     To print files simultaneously for easy viewing use pr(1).

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     The command

     lam file1 file2 file3 file4

     joins 4 files together along each line.  To merge the lines from four
     different files use

     lam file1 -S "\
     " file2 file3 file4

     Every 2 lines of a file may be joined on one line with

     lam - - < file

     and a form letter with substitutions keyed by `@' can be done with

     lam -t @ letter changes

